Z||000002||Capt Fiero||07-30-2001||12:08 PM||||I have used the Delco Rapid fire plugs with a semi stock ignition and they were great, when I say semi stock, I mean MSD Wires and Blaster Coil) I would like to know what all of you guys are using with an ignition box, should I over gap the plugs a bit.  Shawn H. I know you are on here, and your input would really be appreaciated, seeing as I am going to be doing alot to my car that you have already done. (NOS, and Such)<P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=32415"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||07-30-2001||04:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by firstfiero:<BR>[B<P>Need to know what the gap is for 86 se 2.8.<P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>.045 AC Delco RapidFires <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bruce||07-30-2001||07:33 PM||||The Bosch platinum work for me. Just be sure to put anti-sieze on the threads when you install the plugs.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||07-30-2001||07:40 PM||||To each their own, I have had nothing but problems with the Bosch plugs. Even with Fiats, they are the factory plug for those!||||reg||1
Z||000006||grinthock||07-30-2001||07:41 PM||||According to Race-Tech do'nt use the Bosch Platinum's use the RapidFire plugs, although they said the Platinum +4's are OK.  (which I have in my other car they are amazing)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steve Normington||07-30-2001||08:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cozmo:<BR><B>Spark plugs???<BR>Darn... where did my dog run off to?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nobody said anything about Champions, so why is your dog running?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||07-30-2001||08:57 PM||||I have used Bosch Platnums on my V-6. They worked better than a stock plug. This year I tried the AC Delco Rapid Fires. The performance is the same but it does seem to start a little easier with the Rapid Fires.<BR>The center electrode on the rapid fires is also larger in diameter than the bosch platnums. I have never tried a multi-electrode plug (Split fire, Bosch +4's, etc)<BR>But I know that some engines don't like them. If you are running a boosted ignition system I would run the Rapid Fires. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||07-30-2001||10:44 PM||||You need to contact Vinniewolf, the spark plug expert.  LOL,  old thread...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||07-30-2001||11:13 PM||||I have Bosch platinum's in my GT. The only problem I had with them is that one of the plugs had a broken ceramic insulator around the center electrode. I would have just gotten cheap AC's but the platinums were on sale and I figured that I could go a little longer between plug changes with them.||||reg||1
Z||000011||2birds||07-31-2001||12:07 AM||||I've used the original AC Delco, Split Fire (almost embarrassed to admit that), and the Bosch Platinums.  The BP's seem to work best for me.  The Split Fires had MAJOR detonation, no matter what I did with the timing, and the OEM's looked terrible after 20,000 miles.  Mine's a stock V-6, run exclusively on premium.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 07-31-2001).]||||reg||1
